Avalanche Kernel 0.2.0 source, user applications, avalanche.elf and the
Avalanche filesystem image are covered by ../LICENSE (MIT).

avalanche.iso additionally contains unmodified GNU GRUB 2.12-9+deb13u2
binaries from Debian, distributed under the respective licenses in
GRUB-COPYRIGHT. Corresponding upstream source and Debian patches/build
rules are included in grub-source/. GRUB is a separate bootloader, not
part of the Avalanche kernel. The MIT license does not relicense GRUB.

Implemented: x86 ring-3 static ELF32 execution; supervisor/user paging;
checked int 0x80 system calls; user fault termination and CPU budget;
userspace shell, hello, cat, UDP and HTTP programs; PIT and PIC; PCI;
legacy ATA and AHCI SATA; checksummed copy-on-write Avalanche FS v2;
RTL8139 and E1000 Ethernet; ARP, IPv4, ICMP, UDP, DHCP, DNS, and an
active-open TCP stream with an HTTP/1.0 client; PS/2 keyboard/mouse;
COM1 serial, CMOS RTC, PC speaker, and RGB framebuffer console.

Limits: one user process and one TCP connection at a time. No POSIX or
Linux ABI, multiprocess scheduler, dynamic linker, TCP listener, TLS,
IPv6, IP fragmentation/reassembly, native USB, NVMe, Wi-Fi, audio mixer,
or GPU acceleration. DHCP leases require manual renewal. TCP uses
in-order receive, stop-and-wait sending and bounded retries; it does not
implement SACK, window scaling, congestion control or a TIME_WAIT table.
FS v2 has a flat namespace, 32 files of at most 64 KiB each, and requires
its own MBR partition type 0x7f. Without a recognized disk, files use RAM.
AHCI uses the first discovered controller and at most eight SATA ports.
Storage drivers require 512-byte logical sectors.
The page allocator manages usable RAM below 128 MiB. An x86 CPU with PSE
is required. The kernel is 32-bit; the UEFI loader targets x86-64 firmware.
Secure Boot signatures are not supplied. These are development images,
not a complete general-purpose distribution.

The ISO, kernel, user programs and data disk were built. No boot, runtime,
network, storage or automated tests were run at the user's request.
